WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.985 --> 00:00:06.605
- Hello and welcome everyone
to ED Talks with Khan Academy.

00:00:06.605 --> 00:00:09.985
I'm the chief learning
officer here at Khan Academy.

00:00:09.985 --> 00:00:13.515
And today I'm excited to
talk to Mark Sternberg

00:00:13.515 --> 00:00:16.735
who is the K-12 education program director

00:00:16.735 --> 00:00:19.045
at the Walton family foundation.

00:00:19.045 --> 00:00:20.825
So we're looking forward
to that conversation

00:00:20.825 --> 00:00:25.725
to talk about COVID response
in education and philanthropy

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:27.739
as we think about what lies ahead

00:00:27.739 --> 00:00:32.555
for schools after the
results of all of this COVID

00:00:33.535 --> 00:00:35.485
experience that we've all been through.

00:00:36.405 --> 00:00:39.205
Sal is going to be back on Friday,

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:43.055
his guest will be chess world
champion, Magnus Carlsen.

00:00:43.055 --> 00:00:45.435
So look out for that.

00:00:45.435 --> 00:00:47.575
As we think about COVID

00:00:47.575 --> 00:00:52.058
we also want to recognize the sponsors

00:00:53.505 --> 00:00:55.883
who have stepped up and helped us

00:00:55.883 --> 00:00:59.995
make it through the COVID
times here at Khan Academy

00:00:59.995 --> 00:01:04.415
including Bank of
America, AR&amp;T, Google.org,

00:01:04.415 --> 00:01:08.225
Novartis, Fastly, and general motors.

00:01:08.225 --> 00:01:09.798
Thank you for your support.

00:01:10.645 --> 00:01:14.058
And with that, let's dive right in.

00:01:15.695 --> 00:01:17.608
So first let me say welcome.

00:01:18.505 --> 00:01:20.455
Thanks for coming and joining us today.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.745
- It is a pleasure Kristen to be with you.

00:01:23.745 --> 00:01:27.335
I am no international chess champion

00:01:27.335 --> 00:01:30.815
but I'm an education
advocate, par excellence

00:01:30.815 --> 00:01:33.055
and I'll do my best to keep up.

00:01:33.055 --> 00:01:33.957
- Awesome.

00:01:33.957 --> 00:01:34.819
(chuckles)

00:01:34.819 --> 00:01:36.419
We all have our own strengths.

00:01:36.419 --> 00:01:38.075
(chuckles)

00:01:38.075 --> 00:01:39.465
- I mentioned to you off air.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:41.785
I'm, I love chess though.

00:01:41.785 --> 00:01:44.695
So I'll be tuning in on
Friday, you can be sure.

00:01:44.695 --> 00:01:46.885
And hopefully we'll go
through some openings

00:01:46.885 --> 00:01:48.855
or I dunno, end game.

00:01:48.855 --> 00:01:53.125
- Yeah, I can't wait to
hear that conversation,

00:01:53.125 --> 00:01:54.465
I think it'll be good,

00:01:54.465 --> 00:01:58.735
but for everyone that is here today,

00:01:58.735 --> 00:01:59.935
first let's just kick off

00:01:59.935 --> 00:02:02.135
and tell us a little bit about yourself.

00:02:02.135 --> 00:02:05.199
Tell us how you ended up in this position,

00:02:05.199 --> 00:02:07.748
in your, a bit of your path to get here.

00:02:08.735 --> 00:02:11.935
- Great, well, I'm happy
too, and in all seriousness

00:02:11.935 --> 00:02:14.495
thank you for having me and thank you,

00:02:14.495 --> 00:02:15.502
Kristen to you, to Sarah

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:19.245
and to the entire Khan
Academy family and team

00:02:19.245 --> 00:02:22.145
for the amazing work you guys
have done for so many years

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:24.345
and especially frankly over the last year.

00:02:24.345 --> 00:02:27.105
I think we'll wind up talking a bit that

00:02:27.105 --> 00:02:28.155
in our time together.

00:02:29.545 --> 00:02:34.545
My path into this role
runs through the classroom,

00:02:37.575 --> 00:02:40.068
right out of college
I took a job teaching.

00:02:41.725 --> 00:02:43.945
I work for the Walton family foundation

00:02:43.945 --> 00:02:46.395
which is of course is based
in Bentonville, Arkansas.

00:02:46.395 --> 00:02:51.005
I am based in Brooklyn
and I much of my life,

00:02:51.005 --> 00:02:52.875
my professional life
before coming to Walton

00:02:52.875 --> 00:02:55.825
was in service, public
service, Tuesday city

00:02:55.825 --> 00:03:00.155
and the teacher right out of
college as a school leader,

00:03:00.155 --> 00:03:03.165
a school founder started a high school

00:03:03.165 --> 00:03:05.005
that I led for six years in the Bronx,

00:03:05.005 --> 00:03:08.115
the Bronx lab school,
and then as a policymaker

00:03:08.115 --> 00:03:10.615
serving as a deputy to chance for crime

00:03:10.615 --> 00:03:12.765
in the last term that the
Bloomberg administration.

00:03:12.765 --> 00:03:17.765
So my, I cut my teeth as
a pedagog as a pedagogue,

00:03:21.135 --> 00:03:22.905
working with a group of students

00:03:22.905 --> 00:03:25.905
then with the school community

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:30.565
and then in service in New
York city at the policy level

00:03:30.565 --> 00:03:33.565
all of which have been
kind of great preparation

00:03:33.565 --> 00:03:36.805
for this work with the Waltons

00:03:36.805 --> 00:03:39.155
thinking about education
on the national stage.

00:03:40.825 --> 00:03:42.575
- That's great.

00:03:42.575 --> 00:03:45.605
Tell us just a little bit
about founding a school.

00:03:45.605 --> 00:03:49.925
What prompted that and
what were you thinking

00:03:49.925 --> 00:03:52.565
as you were thinking, this
is a different experience

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.835
or a new way of thinking about school

00:03:54.835 --> 00:03:56.129
that you wanted to take?

00:03:56.129 --> 00:04:01.129
- Well, it came from a lot of,

00:04:02.895 --> 00:04:06.308
a lot of it came from a
desire to have impact,

00:04:07.215 --> 00:04:11.705
which was, is a little bit in my DNA

00:04:11.705 --> 00:04:13.638
of sort of just being a doer,

00:04:15.895 --> 00:04:20.895
kind of the match that
lit the fire as it were

00:04:21.565 --> 00:04:23.375
for the school was my teaching experience

00:04:23.375 --> 00:04:27.775
where I sort of observed what educators

00:04:27.775 --> 00:04:29.515
many educators in your community

00:04:29.515 --> 00:04:33.165
I'm sure feel incredible passion

00:04:33.165 --> 00:04:35.915
and energy and potential of my students

00:04:36.845 --> 00:04:39.795
who probably taught me
a lot more in my years

00:04:39.795 --> 00:04:42.255
of teaching than I,
especially those early years.

00:04:42.255 --> 00:04:47.255
And I was able to teach
them a sense of wanting

00:04:50.057 --> 00:04:52.665
to build a school community in the Bronx

00:04:52.665 --> 00:04:57.405
especially that would
be there for students

00:04:57.405 --> 00:04:59.715
and help them meet their full potential.

00:04:59.715 --> 00:05:02.155
And I think fair to say,

00:05:02.155 --> 00:05:04.705
starting teaching in the
mid nineties, in the Bronx

00:05:05.895 --> 00:05:08.515
a sense that we could just do better,

00:05:08.515 --> 00:05:11.605
a sense that schools were,

00:05:11.605 --> 00:05:15.578
too often mixed about how
they were making decisions.

00:05:16.905 --> 00:05:19.335
My school had three principals in the,

00:05:19.335 --> 00:05:22.235
the years I was there.

00:05:22.235 --> 00:05:25.415
So I experienced kind of on the one hand,

00:05:25.415 --> 00:05:28.275
this radical sense of
potential for my students

00:05:28.275 --> 00:05:30.415
who were just waiting in a sense.

00:05:30.415 --> 00:05:34.135
And so I experienced the wonders of that,

00:05:34.135 --> 00:05:37.435
but also the tragedy of that and felt

00:05:37.435 --> 00:05:41.515
that coming there was a brief
interlude of grad school

00:05:41.515 --> 00:05:44.825
between teaching and coming
back to start the school.

00:05:44.825 --> 00:05:49.125
And I felt like, how was my calling?

00:05:49.125 --> 00:05:52.845
I was, I was called back to come

00:05:52.845 --> 00:05:55.595
and try to craft something very different

00:05:55.595 --> 00:05:57.765
for high school students.

00:05:57.765 --> 00:05:58.995
I have a high school student in,

00:05:58.995 --> 00:06:01.599
my own is walking into the room right now.

00:06:01.599 --> 00:06:02.833
(laughs)

00:06:02.833 --> 00:06:06.267
- Welcome life during COVID times, yeah.

00:06:06.267 --> 00:06:07.525
(chuckles)

00:06:07.525 --> 00:06:10.475
- So hopefully, yeah, just keep
it a real for your viewers.

00:06:12.173 --> 00:06:17.088
The, this was also a
moment closing the door,

00:06:18.296 --> 00:06:23.296
that a moment of remarkable
kind of policy, opportunity

00:06:23.635 --> 00:06:25.925
and and a window of leadership

00:06:25.925 --> 00:06:28.008
for New York city and it's schools that,

00:06:29.395 --> 00:06:34.395
I view as a singular
where, the city's mayor

00:06:35.375 --> 00:06:39.435
and chancer looked out at
the landscape of options

00:06:39.435 --> 00:06:43.505
and saw a cohort of schools
that have been failing students

00:06:43.505 --> 00:06:48.445
for decades with graduation
rates in the teens and twenties

00:06:48.445 --> 00:06:51.985
and basically like sick
and tired of being sick

00:06:51.985 --> 00:06:55.095
and tired and, and looking
for a of entrepreneurs

00:06:55.095 --> 00:06:59.058
to come in and think about
a better path forward.

00:07:00.133 --> 00:07:03.385
- Yeah, that definitely makes sense.

00:07:03.385 --> 00:07:06.284
Tell me a little bit
more about your role now

00:07:06.284 --> 00:07:10.825
at the foundation and what
that entails, what you do?

00:07:12.035 --> 00:07:16.265
- Well, education's been a huge pillar

00:07:16.265 --> 00:07:20.345
of the Walton family's
philanthropic interest

00:07:20.345 --> 00:07:23.665
for 30 years long before I got here.

00:07:23.665 --> 00:07:26.278
And far into the future,

00:07:27.585 --> 00:07:30.335
the family has cared about this work.

00:07:30.335 --> 00:07:34.835
It's been the center of
their philanthropic strategy

00:07:34.835 --> 00:07:39.385
and that's really about a belief.

00:07:39.385 --> 00:07:44.385
First is education matters
that it is a central pillar

00:07:44.585 --> 00:07:47.675
and helping communities and families

00:07:47.675 --> 00:07:51.155
and students live, a self-determined life

00:07:51.155 --> 00:07:52.725
and reach their full potential

00:07:54.115 --> 00:07:59.115
and a sense that so much
of what we can do is unlock

00:07:59.525 --> 00:08:03.673
the potential both the
students and of educators.

00:08:03.673 --> 00:08:08.673
The center of our strategy
is looking to teachers

00:08:09.415 --> 00:08:11.755
and looking to educators, looking to folks

00:08:11.755 --> 00:08:13.265
who know the work the best

00:08:14.105 --> 00:08:14.938
because they've done it,

00:08:14.938 --> 00:08:17.615
and they've lived it for a round of ideas

00:08:17.615 --> 00:08:20.505
about how schools and
classrooms can work differently.

00:08:20.505 --> 00:08:22.185
And so it's really been the privilege

00:08:22.185 --> 00:08:25.098
of a lifetime to be in a position to help,

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:27.798
help the family do its work.

00:08:31.115 --> 00:08:32.755
- So tell me a little
bit about what you do.

00:08:32.755 --> 00:08:37.262
So are you looking for opportunities

00:08:37.262 --> 00:08:40.505
and groups too that you wanna support?

00:08:40.505 --> 00:08:42.355
What does that look like?

00:08:42.355 --> 00:08:47.355
- Yeah, well, so we,
what we are focused on,

00:08:51.415 --> 00:08:53.965
I would say a few key pillars.

00:08:53.965 --> 00:08:58.965
One at the center of
our work is the school

00:08:59.135 --> 00:09:00.375
and a quality school.

00:09:00.375 --> 00:09:02.072
So a lot of what we've done over the years

00:09:02.072 --> 00:09:05.705
and will continue to do is find educators

00:09:05.705 --> 00:09:07.685
who have ideas of a
different kind of school

00:09:07.685 --> 00:09:10.735
where the school could
be a new charter school

00:09:10.735 --> 00:09:12.135
a new public school

00:09:12.135 --> 00:09:14.775
but elevating talented educators

00:09:14.775 --> 00:09:17.925
who have in mind to to start,

00:09:17.925 --> 00:09:20.345
either to start a school
or to become school leaders

00:09:20.345 --> 00:09:23.045
and find their path to school leadership.

00:09:23.045 --> 00:09:27.198
Of course, that is important.

00:09:28.245 --> 00:09:31.415
It requires cohorts of educators,

00:09:31.415 --> 00:09:33.305
folks who wanna come into the profession.

00:09:33.305 --> 00:09:36.775
So investing pipelines
into the profession,

00:09:36.775 --> 00:09:40.935
investing in community
advocacy and community voice,

00:09:40.935 --> 00:09:44.428
so to help communities develop a vision

00:09:44.428 --> 00:09:47.655
and a voice, what school can look like,

00:09:47.655 --> 00:09:51.365
how school can meet them, meet
the needs of their community

00:09:51.365 --> 00:09:54.778
which I think in this
moment of of all moments,

00:09:55.938 --> 00:09:58.428
is critically important.

00:09:59.943 --> 00:10:04.943
And I think, also critical at this moment

00:10:06.315 --> 00:10:09.325
is an investment in kind
of what the future of

00:10:09.325 --> 00:10:10.375
learning can look like

00:10:10.375 --> 00:10:12.485
what the future of the classroom can be.

00:10:12.485 --> 00:10:17.065
So a deep focus, we apply a deep focus

00:10:17.065 --> 00:10:20.245
on kind of broadly innovation

00:10:20.245 --> 00:10:21.585
and teaching and innovation

00:10:21.585 --> 00:10:26.518
and models and what the
future can look like.

00:10:29.375 --> 00:10:32.045
- Their lots to follow up on there.

00:10:32.045 --> 00:10:35.435
So let me start with the thinking about,

00:10:35.435 --> 00:10:38.239
you said, especially in these times,

00:10:38.239 --> 00:10:42.475
and as we think about as COVID hit,

00:10:42.475 --> 00:10:45.135
what did you see as,

00:10:45.135 --> 00:10:47.505
how you could bring your resources

00:10:47.505 --> 00:10:50.852
to what was happening
as COVID started in just

00:10:50.852 --> 00:10:53.075
about a year ago this week,

00:10:53.075 --> 00:10:55.525
we started to see a lot of impact.

00:10:57.775 --> 00:11:00.698
- Well, I mean, our first instinct,

00:11:01.575 --> 00:11:03.735
I think, like everybody
it took us a minute

00:11:03.735 --> 00:11:06.448
to kind of understand the scope of this.

00:11:08.725 --> 00:11:13.615
The fact that we now like at the end

00:11:14.465 --> 00:11:17.015
and I hope passed the point of view about,

00:11:19.875 --> 00:11:22.175
like how many days a week,
or it's going to be in school

00:11:22.175 --> 00:11:24.095
or when can we get kids
back into the building?

00:11:24.095 --> 00:11:27.765
I mean, I'm living in New York city

00:11:27.765 --> 00:11:31.935
and what I can report to
your listeners in this town,

00:11:31.935 --> 00:11:34.615
it's, we're sort of seeing the other side

00:11:34.615 --> 00:11:35.565
of that discussion,

00:11:37.162 --> 00:11:40.325
but at the beginning I
think we all thought maybe

00:11:40.325 --> 00:11:44.385
like you digging this was gonna be muddle

00:11:44.385 --> 00:11:45.972
through for the rest of this

00:11:47.435 --> 00:11:48.915
so that we're now talking right.

00:11:48.915 --> 00:11:51.375
We're deep into the
second full school year

00:11:52.635 --> 00:11:54.972
of confronting us is just our,

00:11:58.665 --> 00:12:03.095
I would characterize the work we have done

00:12:03.095 --> 00:12:06.348
in the wake of COVID in parts.

00:12:08.055 --> 00:12:12.165
First was kind of just
trying to be a resource

00:12:12.165 --> 00:12:16.020
for leaders in folks we've worked with

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:18.925
and the cities in which we've worked.

00:12:18.925 --> 00:12:22.615
So providing some
flexibility around resources

00:12:22.615 --> 00:12:26.035
and even some additional
resources dollars.

00:12:26.035 --> 00:12:30.305
So that school leaders can do the things

00:12:30.305 --> 00:12:32.977
that frankly the people we're
privileged to work with.

00:12:32.977 --> 00:12:35.425
We found them doing like
delivering books and material

00:12:35.425 --> 00:12:39.148
and delivering meals, making sure that,

00:12:40.055 --> 00:12:43.775
families in their community had,

00:12:43.775 --> 00:12:48.565
hotspots to get online when
instruction moved online.

00:12:48.565 --> 00:12:51.255
So just the first order of business for us

00:12:51.255 --> 00:12:56.255
was just providing extra
resource to meet the moment.

00:12:56.725 --> 00:12:59.905
And there was, we knew we
could never do everything

00:12:59.905 --> 00:13:01.475
but we needed to do something.

00:13:01.475 --> 00:13:04.362
So that was, that was one is that.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:09.145
- Yeah, and that extra
hotspots that's been for us,

00:13:09.145 --> 00:13:11.705
because obviously we are

00:13:11.705 --> 00:13:14.985
an education technology organization

00:13:14.985 --> 00:13:18.225
and we struggle with this,

00:13:18.225 --> 00:13:22.355
part of equity is actually
getting the resources

00:13:22.355 --> 00:13:24.105
to the students who need it most.

00:13:24.105 --> 00:13:28.555
And we, are always thinking about,

00:13:28.555 --> 00:13:31.355
gosh there's this whole group
of students we can't reach

00:13:31.355 --> 00:13:33.585
because they don't have that connectivity.

00:13:33.585 --> 00:13:36.068
So have you guys been
working on that issue?

00:13:37.805 --> 00:13:40.245
- We have, and that's sort of a bridge

00:13:41.285 --> 00:13:43.505
to the second big part of what we've done.

00:13:43.505 --> 00:13:44.785
And that's sort of thinking

00:13:44.785 --> 00:13:46.855
about through an advocacy lens,

00:13:46.855 --> 00:13:49.645
thinking about how we
can address the needs

00:13:49.645 --> 00:13:51.555
that became so clear
and acute at the moment.

00:13:51.555 --> 00:13:54.278
And at the top of that list was access,

00:13:55.305 --> 00:14:00.305
access to clear broadband
and kind of connectivity.

00:14:04.105 --> 00:14:07.385
And even though I think
we've got good news

00:14:07.385 --> 00:14:10.015
coming on this Christian,

00:14:10.015 --> 00:14:12.525
I think my understanding is that

00:14:12.525 --> 00:14:14.605
the legislation that is making its way

00:14:14.605 --> 00:14:17.265
to the president's desk includes

00:14:17.265 --> 00:14:20.095
some good for families that are living

00:14:20.095 --> 00:14:22.015
on the wrong side of digital tracks.

00:14:22.015 --> 00:14:23.765
So that's encouraging

00:14:23.765 --> 00:14:25.688
and we're ready to celebrate that.

00:14:28.135 --> 00:14:31.265
It's also, I think you'd agree,

00:14:31.265 --> 00:14:36.095
a moment to just pause
and sort of acknowledge

00:14:36.095 --> 00:14:39.875
that there are still
families in this country

00:14:39.875 --> 00:14:42.905
that have, it's not as
they don't have access

00:14:42.905 --> 00:14:44.615
to high-speed internet.

00:14:44.615 --> 00:14:47.135
They have lived through the last 12 months

00:14:47.135 --> 00:14:48.535
without high-speed internet.

00:14:51.405 --> 00:14:53.508
And I'd like to think that there is,

00:14:56.348 --> 00:14:58.125
I'm thrilled that there's a solution.

00:14:58.125 --> 00:15:00.035
And we know that just because

00:15:00.035 --> 00:15:02.965
there's shuttle resource where
this problem is not solved

00:15:02.965 --> 00:15:05.865
there there's a lot of
work to do to get this,

00:15:05.865 --> 00:15:08.075
to get those dollars to the right places

00:15:08.075 --> 00:15:10.175
and to build solutions that are permanent.

00:15:11.285 --> 00:15:13.528
I guess my reflection on this is that,

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:21.790
this experience has raised
awareness of problems like this.

00:15:23.465 --> 00:15:27.225
And I think generated a lot of public will

00:15:27.225 --> 00:15:28.215
to solve this problems.

00:15:28.215 --> 00:15:31.665
I guess what I wanna
express to your viewers,

00:15:31.665 --> 00:15:36.055
is I hope we can hold on to the urgency.

00:15:36.055 --> 00:15:40.525
We feel that we don't
sort of get back to life

00:15:40.525 --> 00:15:44.655
as usual and forget the urgency
of many of these challenges

00:15:44.655 --> 00:15:46.128
which frankly, before COVID,

00:15:47.975 --> 00:15:49.735
well let's just say they pre-dated COVID

00:15:49.735 --> 00:15:52.251
and we tolerated them before COVID,

00:15:52.251 --> 00:15:55.715
so as we moved back to life, life as usual

00:15:55.715 --> 00:15:56.915
I hope that we can hold on to,

00:15:56.915 --> 00:15:59.815
kind of the urgency of solving
some of these big problems.

00:16:01.075 --> 00:16:02.105
- Definitely.

00:16:02.105 --> 00:16:05.235
So thinking about that future,

00:16:05.235 --> 00:16:08.715
what do you think as we look forward

00:16:08.715 --> 00:16:11.035
are some of the things that you see

00:16:11.035 --> 00:16:14.128
may change perfectly in
education from where we are now?

00:16:14.995 --> 00:16:17.745
- Well, I think I'd
make a few points here.

00:16:17.745 --> 00:16:19.565
I do think that this is a moment to grab

00:16:19.565 --> 00:16:22.565
for a different kind of
future in the classroom.

00:16:22.565 --> 00:16:26.565
And that future has always been something

00:16:26.565 --> 00:16:27.685
we've been able to talk about

00:16:27.685 --> 00:16:29.625
as long as I've been in the work

00:16:29.625 --> 00:16:31.625
from when I was a classroom teacher

00:16:31.625 --> 00:16:35.945
to a school leader, to a policy guy,

00:16:35.945 --> 00:16:38.965
but it has always been
just over the horizon

00:16:38.965 --> 00:16:41.735
and I would credit Khan Academy and Sol,

00:16:41.735 --> 00:16:43.565
and all of your collective work

00:16:43.565 --> 00:16:47.555
is helping us kind of
like see it more clearly.

00:16:47.555 --> 00:16:49.450
But I think you'd agree.

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:51.988
It's just been like right over the edge.

00:16:52.865 --> 00:16:55.105
- As someone who's been in for four years

00:16:55.105 --> 00:16:56.695
that I wanna say yes,

00:16:56.695 --> 00:16:58.923
it has always been right over the edge.

00:16:58.923 --> 00:17:00.055
(chuckles)

00:17:00.055 --> 00:17:02.495
- Well, so, I mean,
just generally speaking

00:17:02.495 --> 00:17:05.605
this needs to be a moment when we grab it

00:17:05.605 --> 00:17:08.215
and we grab it and run toward it,

00:17:08.215 --> 00:17:11.135
where like we understand it,

00:17:11.135 --> 00:17:15.435
not as something nice to have, but a

00:17:15.435 --> 00:17:19.485
and necessity for the equity agenda

00:17:19.485 --> 00:17:21.785
for the work of like honestly lifting up

00:17:21.785 --> 00:17:22.755
the teaching profession.

00:17:22.755 --> 00:17:24.877
I mean, you've seen the data Christian,

00:17:24.877 --> 00:17:26.570
we'll see if it holds the right now

00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:30.325
one in five teachers are telling us

00:17:30.325 --> 00:17:31.985
they're not coming back to the classroom.

00:17:31.985 --> 00:17:36.985
And that's already like on
top of a moment for a decade

00:17:37.001 --> 00:17:40.885
when one of the critical
challenges is attracting

00:17:40.885 --> 00:17:44.425
and retaining quality
talent in the classroom.

00:17:44.425 --> 00:17:47.905
So we've got to make the
profession more sustainable

00:17:47.905 --> 00:17:51.348
and kind of help teachers
be more effective.

00:17:52.371 --> 00:17:55.055
We gotta like lift up student learning.

00:17:55.055 --> 00:18:00.055
Like there are big leaps
forward that need to happen.

00:18:00.771 --> 00:18:04.215
If we're gonna help
schools meet their promise

00:18:04.215 --> 00:18:08.285
and software based solutions
need to be a part of that.

00:18:08.285 --> 00:18:11.575
Absolutely and I think a
close observer might say

00:18:11.575 --> 00:18:15.945
that despite some great
progress over the last decade

00:18:15.945 --> 00:18:19.575
it just hasn't happened in some
ways teachers are telling us

00:18:19.575 --> 00:18:21.765
that the profession has
gotten harder, not easier.

00:18:21.765 --> 00:18:24.995
So this is a moment we've
got a going to reach

00:18:24.995 --> 00:18:26.525
over that horizon.

00:18:26.525 --> 00:18:29.765
You develop solutions, look
to teachers and entrepreneurs

00:18:29.765 --> 00:18:32.265
to help us figure out what those are

00:18:32.265 --> 00:18:34.425
and bring more coherence to the

00:18:34.425 --> 00:18:35.995
the teaching and learning experience.

00:18:35.995 --> 00:18:40.525
So that, I mean, I'm speaking
maybe more in generalities

00:18:40.525 --> 00:18:41.358
than you'd like me to,

00:18:41.358 --> 00:18:45.488
but that's our thesis and
we're excited to do our part.

00:18:46.395 --> 00:18:49.065
- Yeah, so we've got a
question that kind of leads

00:18:49.065 --> 00:18:52.645
to that from Facebook, Lori,
Beth Ford, Gerald says,

00:18:52.645 --> 00:18:57.275
please define success for
students, teachers in schools.

00:18:57.275 --> 00:18:59.265
And I think that's an interesting question

00:18:59.265 --> 00:19:00.705
to think about what is,

00:19:00.705 --> 00:19:03.655
if we talk about wanting
to see the changes

00:19:03.655 --> 00:19:05.175
in teachers and students

00:19:05.175 --> 00:19:08.298
and what is success, how
do we define success?

00:19:10.035 --> 00:19:15.028
- Well, I mean, for us,
that success is about,

00:19:17.375 --> 00:19:19.065
this idea of self-determination

00:19:19.065 --> 00:19:21.445
that school's going to prepare you for,

00:19:21.445 --> 00:19:23.068
to lead the life you wanna lead.

00:19:24.335 --> 00:19:26.414
And I think that's got to be

00:19:26.414 --> 00:19:29.005
at the student level,

00:19:29.005 --> 00:19:32.125
we've got to be focused
on skill development

00:19:32.125 --> 00:19:35.245
and did all things kind of habits of mind

00:19:35.245 --> 00:19:40.245
and content mastery and basic skills

00:19:42.185 --> 00:19:44.437
and numeracy and literacy

00:19:44.437 --> 00:19:48.515
and critical thinking
that prepare students for,

00:19:48.515 --> 00:19:49.348
can you hear me?

00:19:51.625 --> 00:19:52.795
- Yeah, you're good.

00:19:52.795 --> 00:19:53.795
- Okay, good, sorry.

00:19:56.115 --> 00:20:01.115
So skill development and
mastery, content mastery.

00:20:04.435 --> 00:20:07.255
I think we also need
to attend to the social

00:20:07.255 --> 00:20:10.675
and emotional health of
our students as well.

00:20:10.675 --> 00:20:13.368
I will say that,

00:20:14.355 --> 00:20:18.985
the last year has been a massive reminder

00:20:18.985 --> 00:20:22.335
and I'll say more candidly, a set back,

00:20:22.335 --> 00:20:24.885
for the children in this country.

00:20:24.885 --> 00:20:27.835
And we're gonna have to confront

00:20:27.835 --> 00:20:32.805
that as a country and put front and center

00:20:35.125 --> 00:20:38.335
kind of the emotional
wellbeing of students.

00:20:38.335 --> 00:20:40.175
When we think about success.

00:20:40.175 --> 00:20:41.625
Now, I'd like to believe that

00:20:43.655 --> 00:20:46.305
if these things are
not mutually exclusive,

00:20:46.305 --> 00:20:48.495
if the idea of developing hard skills

00:20:49.605 --> 00:20:52.565
that we know are important
for a life well lab

00:20:52.565 --> 00:20:55.125
for this idea of self-determination

00:20:56.335 --> 00:21:00.030
of being literate and numerate
at the highest levels,

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:04.125
are actually quite
consistent with the idea

00:21:04.125 --> 00:21:07.988
of like emotional wealth
of health and wellbeing.

00:21:08.915 --> 00:21:11.845
So I think, when I think
about student success

00:21:11.845 --> 00:21:13.648
those are the things I think about.

00:21:13.648 --> 00:21:16.345
When I think about teacher success,

00:21:16.345 --> 00:21:20.065
I think about moving educators

00:21:20.065 --> 00:21:24.145
to a place where they focused exclusively

00:21:24.145 --> 00:21:27.865
on human centered elements of pedagogy,

00:21:27.865 --> 00:21:32.085
where we are developing
tools in the classroom

00:21:32.085 --> 00:21:34.595
that work harder for teachers
that make their giant

00:21:34.595 --> 00:21:36.518
their jobs and their lives easier.

00:21:37.775 --> 00:21:42.775
They get them faster to the work

00:21:43.395 --> 00:21:46.155
of supporting student learning

00:21:48.235 --> 00:21:50.568
which is why the teacher
joins the profession.

00:21:51.895 --> 00:21:55.155
So if we can do that,
they're more satisfied,

00:21:55.155 --> 00:21:56.415
they're more effective.

00:21:56.415 --> 00:21:59.245
Their time and energy are
more focused in centered

00:21:59.245 --> 00:22:04.075
on the things that are of highest value.

00:22:04.075 --> 00:22:06.415
And I think we're, I
think if we're doing that

00:22:06.415 --> 00:22:08.368
at scale or lifting the profession up,

00:22:09.915 --> 00:22:13.478
so those are the first
thoughts that come to mind.

00:22:14.375 --> 00:22:17.045
- Yeah, you talked
about self-determination

00:22:17.045 --> 00:22:18.755
for students doing what they wanna do.

00:22:18.755 --> 00:22:22.295
I also know that in some of
the things that you talk about

00:22:22.295 --> 00:22:25.275
in terms of the types of
innovations, you're looking for

00:22:25.275 --> 00:22:29.438
you talk about community
driven or community demand.

00:22:30.535 --> 00:22:32.445
How do you bring that

00:22:32.445 --> 00:22:33.945
into the work that you do

00:22:33.945 --> 00:22:38.598
and how do you hear the
community demand or listen to it?

00:22:40.525 --> 00:22:41.835
- Small conversations

00:22:43.164 --> 00:22:46.178
that are like kitchen table,

00:22:47.635 --> 00:22:52.635
discussions with folks who
have the most at stake,

00:22:52.995 --> 00:22:54.605
who are closest to the action

00:22:56.475 --> 00:22:57.695
in their communities,

00:22:57.695 --> 00:23:01.385
whether it's in the South
or in the Pacific Northwest.

00:23:01.385 --> 00:23:05.665
And we know there's lots in
common across communities,

00:23:05.665 --> 00:23:08.745
but every, all of these,
all of these problems

00:23:08.745 --> 00:23:09.578
are super local.

00:23:09.578 --> 00:23:13.939
So for us, this has been
about building a muscle

00:23:13.939 --> 00:23:18.485
that is a listening muscle

00:23:20.095 --> 00:23:21.742
which doesn't mean that
we're leaving our experience

00:23:21.742 --> 00:23:25.795
and our points of view behind
the things that we've learned.

00:23:25.795 --> 00:23:27.335
We're in fact, bringing those with us

00:23:27.335 --> 00:23:28.445
into these conversations,

00:23:28.445 --> 00:23:30.055
but we're, this is about,

00:23:30.055 --> 00:23:33.205
I mean this is classic organizing

00:23:33.205 --> 00:23:35.465
and I will say as someone who's been

00:23:35.465 --> 00:23:37.015
in philanthropy a little while,

00:23:38.685 --> 00:23:41.765
well I've said it, a skill
that we've got to build

00:23:41.765 --> 00:23:44.145
it is not the natural
instinct of philanthropy

00:23:44.145 --> 00:23:45.978
to listen first and act second.

00:23:47.665 --> 00:23:52.665
So that's the idea sort of a
go slow to go fast, Kristen.

00:23:56.385 --> 00:23:59.405
- Yeah Yeah, that makes sense.

00:23:59.405 --> 00:24:01.135
I know that when you listen

00:24:01.135 --> 00:24:03.575
sometimes you hear lots
of conflicting viewpoints.

00:24:03.575 --> 00:24:05.695
Like I think the school reopening

00:24:05.695 --> 00:24:07.585
has it been a case in point

00:24:07.585 --> 00:24:09.565
about that where different people

00:24:09.565 --> 00:24:11.405
have really different views

00:24:11.405 --> 00:24:13.965
and think about things in different ways

00:24:13.965 --> 00:24:15.555
and you have different stakeholders

00:24:15.555 --> 00:24:16.858
with different interests.

00:24:17.905 --> 00:24:20.075
Do you, how do you navigate that

00:24:20.075 --> 00:24:22.248
and kind of try to bring people together?

00:24:25.065 --> 00:24:25.898
- I mean, yeah.

00:24:25.898 --> 00:24:29.018
I mean, this is the trick and the risk,

00:24:31.164 --> 00:24:34.415
a lot of good examples of well
intentioned change agendas

00:24:34.415 --> 00:24:37.208
that fall apart because they reach for,

00:24:38.465 --> 00:24:40.475
absolute kumbaya consensus.

00:24:40.475 --> 00:24:43.005
And that's, that can't
be what this is about.

00:24:43.005 --> 00:24:44.168
I think what we,

00:24:45.125 --> 00:24:47.275
this is about balancing
the patients listening

00:24:47.275 --> 00:24:48.828
with the urgency of action,

00:24:50.515 --> 00:24:55.515
and an orientation on a set
of a set of problems that,

00:25:01.072 --> 00:25:04.845
that can be solved and that
left unsolved have a kind

00:25:04.845 --> 00:25:06.978
of cascading pernicious effect.

00:25:08.625 --> 00:25:13.485
So it's not, this is not an exercise of,

00:25:13.485 --> 00:25:15.485
everybody's standing in
a circle holding hands

00:25:15.485 --> 00:25:17.365
and agreeing on everything.

00:25:17.365 --> 00:25:20.675
This is about, building an understanding

00:25:20.675 --> 00:25:23.225
of the local nature of the problem

00:25:23.225 --> 00:25:25.645
in this case, in your instance,

00:25:25.645 --> 00:25:27.065
how do we get kids back into school?

00:25:27.065 --> 00:25:27.898
And if we can't,

00:25:27.898 --> 00:25:30.895
how do we do everything we can

00:25:30.895 --> 00:25:33.395
to make sure that on
the other side of this,

00:25:33.395 --> 00:25:37.625
we're not confronting and
a learning loss challenge

00:25:37.625 --> 00:25:39.485
that we can't surmount.

00:25:39.485 --> 00:25:42.085
Broadly I mean, that is the
micro version of the problem

00:25:42.085 --> 00:25:44.085
that Walton has been solving for 30 years.

00:25:44.085 --> 00:25:47.082
How can we get a better school

00:25:47.082 --> 00:25:48.845
and a better educational experience

00:25:48.845 --> 00:25:51.255
in reach for more students
in this community?

00:25:51.255 --> 00:25:53.795
We're clear that that's our orientation

00:25:53.795 --> 00:25:54.895
and we're looking for,

00:25:57.545 --> 00:26:00.055
we're looking for local
partners who have frankly,

00:26:00.055 --> 00:26:02.128
better ideas about how to do that,

00:26:03.055 --> 00:26:05.845
because they're close to the problem

00:26:05.845 --> 00:26:07.235
and they've lived it in their homes.

00:26:07.235 --> 00:26:10.515
And we hope that they can invite

00:26:10.515 --> 00:26:14.508
this in to be a resources if
they try to solve that problem.

00:26:16.525 --> 00:26:21.525
- Great, so you talked about
innovation and community,

00:26:21.965 --> 00:26:24.625
tell us some things that
you're excited about

00:26:24.625 --> 00:26:28.365
or some innovative
things that you're seeing

00:26:28.365 --> 00:26:31.115
or working on that excites you.

00:26:31.115 --> 00:26:36.115
- Well, I'll say, let
me start with assessment

00:26:40.005 --> 00:26:45.005
and like, kind of accountability,

00:26:45.945 --> 00:26:47.652
I hesitate to mention the words

00:26:47.652 --> 00:26:50.543
like it's like a
four-letter word these days.

00:26:50.543 --> 00:26:52.793
(chuckles)

00:26:54.834 --> 00:26:59.834
I'll just name this as a vertical
that requires new thinking

00:27:00.714 --> 00:27:05.275
and like a deep attention
to what comes next.

00:27:05.275 --> 00:27:09.815
And I, what I'm very
clear on that is frankly

00:27:09.815 --> 00:27:11.545
keeping me up at night and so many hours

00:27:11.545 --> 00:27:15.365
to get up at night is just
how perilous the moment is

00:27:15.365 --> 00:27:18.058
for the country, as it
relates to data collection,

00:27:20.398 --> 00:27:23.405
student performance, I
mean even absent COVID,

00:27:23.405 --> 00:27:28.405
we're confronting, decision
points at the state

00:27:28.705 --> 00:27:32.285
and federal level of whether
we should be in the business

00:27:32.285 --> 00:27:35.355
of collecting students
data that allows us to have

00:27:35.355 --> 00:27:38.345
kind of comparability to know how one set

00:27:38.345 --> 00:27:41.365
of students is doing relative
to another set of students.

00:27:41.365 --> 00:27:42.865
And then COVID happens.

00:27:42.865 --> 00:27:45.475
And we're now looking at
two consecutive school years

00:27:45.475 --> 00:27:48.835
where we're not sure what
kind of data we're gonna have.

00:27:48.835 --> 00:27:53.835
I believe that is a massive
threat to the equity agenda

00:27:54.954 --> 00:27:58.305
which, there just aren't
many other threats

00:27:58.305 --> 00:28:00.398
that rise to that level of urgency.

00:28:01.945 --> 00:28:05.388
This to me is an innovation challenge.

00:28:06.405 --> 00:28:08.525
We are having a fight in this country

00:28:09.755 --> 00:28:14.645
in the four corners of little
K-12 community, Kristen

00:28:14.645 --> 00:28:16.555
and whether we should test

00:28:16.555 --> 00:28:21.555
and that's an important discussion

00:28:21.615 --> 00:28:23.465
that I hope lands in the right place.

00:28:24.505 --> 00:28:27.415
What we will be doing is
thinking about knowledge

00:28:28.345 --> 00:28:31.035
to capture what students know

00:28:31.035 --> 00:28:34.616
and to capture the full
spectrum of the things

00:28:34.616 --> 00:28:38.025
we care about as it relates
to student performance.

00:28:38.025 --> 00:28:39.915
And this gets back to
your question earlier

00:28:39.915 --> 00:28:43.825
the listener's question, their
social and emotional health,

00:28:43.825 --> 00:28:46.368
their content mastery,
their skill development.

00:28:48.205 --> 00:28:51.445
These are imagined...

